  "slug": "light-computational-experimental-43d6",
  "summary": "Efficient nonparaxial propagation and high-dimensional mode tomography are now validated against analytic and experimental benchmarks.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational treatment of optical field propagation and characterization beyond standard paraxial or idealized assumptions. The Python Fourier-optics framework (arxiv:2611.25116) is benchmarked against analytic Gaussian beam, lens, and grating results, providing a checked simulation tool. The tilted-lens study (arxiv:2611.25427) extends intensity-based spatial mode tomography through an astigmatic transformation and reports fidelity at mode dimension D=66. The convolution-based formulation (arxiv:2611.27232) provides an efficient wide-angle, nonparaxial description of partially coherent light from extended incoherent sources, with absolute radiometric quantities. The common novelty is methodological: each work makes higher-dimensional or less restrictive optical models computationally accessible while checking results against known solutions or measurements.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
